--TEST--
each qualifier bit may be added by an assignment and never dropped by one, on both axes and both bases, and a `tainted` value can never become trusted markup — ADR 0024 §§ 1, 2, 5 and `rule:security/secret-qualifier` and `rule:security/secret-propagation`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The accepted half of this bound -- every widening, the per-axis poisoning
// and the checked-conversion laundering -- is
// tests/conformance/lang/a-tainted-value-answers-what-its-unqualified-twin-does.nvst.
// This file names the first refused step in each direction, so a checker that
// let one bit drop fails here while the accepted half still passes.
tainted string $t = "x";
tainted bytes $tb = "x" as bytes;
secret tainted string $st = "x";

// `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`: dropping `tainted` at an assignment is the hole the qualifier
// exists to close -- without this row a plain-`string` parameter would quietly
// accept a tainted argument at the first call boundary.
string $droppedTaint = $t;

// `rule:types/conversion`: the same rule one base over. `bytes` is not a laundering
// route.
bytes $droppedOnBytes = $tb;

// `rule:security/secret-qualifier`: the two bits are independent, so dropping either one alone is
// refused -- neither axis is a way out of the other.
tainted string $droppedSecret = $st;
secret string $droppedTaintKeptSecret = $st;

// `rule:core-classes/html-auto-escape`: only a source-literal token converts `as Markup`. A tainted
// value is exactly the runtime-computed case that closes the "compute the
// payload, then cast it" bypass.
Core\Html\Markup $forged = $t as Core\Html\Markup;
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:14:24
%A
error[E0401]: expected `bytes`, found `tainted bytes`
   --> case.nvs:18:25
%A
error[E0401]: expected `tainted string`, found `secret tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:22:33
%A
error[E0401]: expected `secret string`, found `secret tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:23:41
%A
error[E0417]: only a string written directly in the code can be converted `as Markup`. A computed or `tainted` value cannot become trusted markup this way
   --> case.nvs:28:28
%A
